#             (which already starts with a leading apostrophe) is appended after the
#             per-context prefix, making both attempts send the wrong SQL]:
#             ROOT CAUSE: The payload variables (e.g. MySQL:
#               "' AND EXTRACTVALUE(1,CONCAT(0x7e,(...),0x7e))-- -"
#             all start with a literal apostrophe hardcoded in the string.  The
#             context-retry loop at lines 51246-51275 prepended an additional prefix:
#               numeric  → _fee_pfx = ""   → original + "" + "'AND..." = still has '
#               string   → _fee_pfx = "'"  → original + "'" + "'AND..." = ''AND...
#             _fee_ctx_prefix (line 51245) was computed correctly as the leading apostrophe
#             character before " AND " in the payload, but the variable was NEVER USED to
#             strip the payload's own prefix before the new prefix was prepended.  Both
#             context attempts therefore sent syntactically broken SQL: the numeric attempt
#             still closed whatever string context the injection was in, and the string
#             attempt doubled the apostrophe (escaping it, preventing string termination).
#             As a result, error_extract_string on a numeric-context injection point ALWAYS
#             failed — every chunk returned an empty body, and "?" was appended for every
#             chunk position, producing a final result of "???...?" × ceil(len/CHUNK_SIZE).
#             Fix: strip the leading apostrophe from the payload string before the
#             context-prefix loop, then reconstruct as _fee_pfx + payload_body.  This
#             ensures:
#               numeric  → "" + "AND EXTRACTVALUE(...)-- -"  (no leading quote, valid for id=1)
#               string   → "'" + "AND EXTRACTVALUE(...)-- -"  (closes the string literal)
#             The variable _fee_ctx_prefix is now used as documented — it drives the strip.
