#   BUG-EXTRACT-STRING-MAXLEN-CAP [_extract_string — when the bisected length equals
#             max_len and max_len >= 64, the length is hardcoded-capped to 32 chars,
#             silently truncating any legitimate string whose length happens to equal
#             the max_len ceiling passed by the caller]:
#             ROOT CAUSE: _extract_string() uses _get_length_fast / _get_length to
#             determine string length via bisection in [0, max_len].  When the oracle
#             returns True for every probe up to max_len (oracle dead — always True),
#             the bisection converges to max_len.  A guard at lines 34007-34010 detected
#             this by checking `_length >= max_len` and capped the result at 32 to
#             prevent extracting garbage up to max_len chars.
#             The guard is ALSO triggered when the actual string length IS max_len
#             (a legitimate 128-char column value extracted with max_len=128, or a
#             256-char value with max_len=256 from the row-dump loop at line 35667).
#             In those cases the oracle is healthy, the length is correct, and the cap
#             at 32 silently truncates real data. Any call that passes max_len=128 for
#             a value of exactly 128 chars hits the cap; max_len=256 for a 256-char
#             row cell (SHA-256 hash in hex = 64 chars is safe; base64url tokens can
#             reach 256 chars) also hits the cap.
#             Fix: add a single confirming probe at LENGTH > max_len before capping.
#             If the oracle says True (length > max_len) it IS broken → cap to 32.
#             If the oracle says False or None (length ≤ max_len) the bisection hit a
#             real boundary → the true length IS max_len → do NOT cap.  One extra
#             HTTP probe per ceiling-hit string (rare) is the cost; incorrect truncation
#             of every full-length string is eliminated.
