#             ROOT CAUSE: SchemaEnumeratorV18.QUERIES["Oracle"]["row_count"] was:
#               SELECT num_rows FROM all_tables WHERE owner='{db}' AND table_name='{table}'
#             all_tables.num_rows is populated by DBMS_STATS.GATHER_TABLE_STATS or
#             ANALYZE TABLE.  For tables that have NEVER had statistics gathered
#             (fresh tables, dev/test environments, after DELETE_TABLE_STATS or before
#             any DBMS_STATS run), num_rows is NULL.  Blind extraction returns "" for a
#             NULL scalar subquery → int("") raises ValueError → get_row_count() catches
#             it and returns 0 → dump_rows() is never called (caller sees "empty table") →
#             zero rows extracted from ALL Oracle tables without fresh statistics.
#             This silently broke every Oracle row dump in SchemaEnumeratorV18 on any
#             Oracle instance that had not run DBMS_STATS (extremely common for dev/test
#             environments and for tables created after the last stats run).
#             Fix (two parts):
#             (1) Changed row_count query to NVL(num_rows, -1) so stale/absent stats
#                 return -1 rather than NULL/empty, making the parse succeed.
#             (2) Updated get_row_count() to detect the -1 sentinel and fall back to an
#                 exact COUNT(*) query (new row_count_exact / row_count_nodb keys) which
#                 is always accurate.  If exact COUNT(*) also fails (no SELECT privilege),
#                 returns 1 so dump_rows() is still called and can attempt extraction.
