#   BUG-COCKROACHDB-ERROR-DUMP-CAST [_error_dump_table — CockroachDB falls through
#             to the generic `else` branch which omits CAST to TEXT, breaking
#             error-based extraction of numeric columns on CockroachDB]:
#             ROOT CAUSE: The cell_query dispatch in _error_dump_table had `elif
#             _dbms == "PostgreSQL"` (not `in ("PostgreSQL", "CockroachDB")`).
#             CockroachDB therefore used the generic else branch:
#               SELECT "{col}" FROM "{table}" LIMIT 1 OFFSET N
#             which passes the raw numeric value (INT/BIGINT/NUMERIC/FLOAT) to the
#             error-extraction template.  The template applies SUBSTRING() to this
#             numeric value; CockroachDB raises "function pg_catalog.substring(integer,
#             integer, integer) does not exist" BEFORE the CAST(... AS INT) error-
#             trigger fires, so extract_char always returns None and every numeric
#             cell is silently returned as "".
#             Fix: extend the PostgreSQL branch to `in ("PostgreSQL", "CockroachDB")`
#             so CockroachDB cells are also wrapped in CAST(... AS TEXT), allowing
#             SUBSTRING to receive a string argument for all column types.
#
#   BUG-SQLITE-ROWCHUNKED-NO-ORDER [SchemaEnumeratorV18 — SQLite row_chunked
#             inner subquery has no ORDER BY, causing non-deterministic pagination
#             across multi-chunk dump calls]:
#             ROOT CAUSE: The SQLite row_chunked template was:
#               SELECT group_concat(...) FROM (SELECT {cols} v_ FROM "{table}"
#               LIMIT {limit} OFFSET {offset})
#             The inner subquery had no ORDER BY clause.  SQLite's LIMIT/OFFSET
#             without ORDER BY returns rows in B-tree physical traversal order,
#             which changes after any INSERT, DELETE, or VACUUM.  For large tables
#             dumped across multiple chunks (chunk 0: OFFSET 0, chunk 1: OFFSET 10,
#             etc.), rows can appear in different orderings between chunk calls if
#             any write happens between calls.  This causes the same row to appear
#             in two consecutive chunks (duplicate), or a row to be skipped because
#             it shifts between chunks.  This is the same root cause as
#             BUG-SQLITE-TABLES-ORDER (tables group_concat without inner ORDER BY)
#             but in the row extraction path rather than the schema enumeration path.
#             Fix: add ORDER BY rowid to the inner subquery.  Every standard SQLite
#             table (without the WITHOUT ROWID modifier) exposes the rowid pseudo-
#             column as a stable, monotonically increasing integer key.  Ordering by
#             rowid gives session-consistent row ordering so each LIMIT/OFFSET page
#             is non-overlapping.  A row_chunked_fallback key is also added using
#             ORDER BY 1 (the projected expression) for the rare WITHOUT ROWID table
#             case where `rowid` is not available.
#
#   BUG-ORACLE-ROW-CHUNKED-INNER-ALIAS [SchemaEnumeratorV18 — all four Oracle
#             row_chunked variants (row_chunked, row_chunked_fallback, row_chunked_nodb,
#             row_chunked_fallback_nodb) are missing a mandatory table alias on the
#             innermost inline view, causing ORA-00933 on pre-12c Oracle targets]:
#             ROOT CAUSE: The existing BUG-ORACLE-ROW-CHUNKED-ALIAS FIX correctly added
#             table aliases _t18_outer (middle subquery) and _ora_agg (outer subquery),
#             but missed the INNERMOST inline view `(SELECT {cols} v_ FROM {db}.{table})`
#             which appears in the FROM clause of the ROWNUM pagination subquery.
#             Oracle pre-12c (9i, 10g, 11g) requires EVERY inline view in a FROM clause
#             to have a table alias.  Without the alias on the innermost subquery, Oracle
#             raises ORA-00933 "SQL command not properly ended" BEFORE the XMLAGG
#             expression is evaluated — completely breaking dump_rows() on any Oracle
#             target running a pre-12c version.  All four template variants (primary,
#             fallback, nodb, fallback_nodb) had the same missing inner alias.
#             Fix: add alias _t18_inner to the innermost `(SELECT {cols} v_ FROM ...)
#             subquery in all four Oracle row_chunked template variants so all three
#             levels of subquery nesting each carry their mandatory table alias.
#
