#               SELECT {col} FROM (SELECT {col}, ROWNUM rn FROM {table}) _t_row WHERE rn={row_idx+1}
#
#   BUG-PG-CTID-VIEW [SchemaEnumeratorV18 — PostgreSQL row_chunked breaks on views and there
#             is no row_chunked_fallback, causing dump_rows() to silently return [] for all
#             PostgreSQL views]:
#             ROOT CAUSE: The primary PostgreSQL row_chunked template uses ORDER BY ctid in
#             the inner subquery.  ctid (physical row locator) is only available on base tables
#             — NOT on PostgreSQL views, which have no physical storage.  When the target table
#             is actually a view, PostgreSQL raises "column ctid does not exist", the extraction
#             returns empty, and dump_rows() falls back to row_chunked_fallback — which did NOT
#             EXIST for PostgreSQL in SchemaEnumeratorV18.QUERIES.  The fallback key returned ""
#             so dump_rows() returned [] silently for every view-based target.
#             Fix: add row_chunked_fallback to PostgreSQL in SchemaEnumeratorV18.QUERIES that
#             uses ORDER BY v_ inside string_agg (no ctid reference), providing correct
#             deterministic ordering for views and base tables alike.
#
#   BUG-SQLITE-TABLES-ORDER [SchemaEnumeratorV18 — SQLite tables query ORDER BY name is a
#             no-op, making get_tables() non-deterministic]:
#             ROOT CAUSE: The original query was:
#               SELECT group_concat(name,',') FROM sqlite_master WHERE type='table' ORDER BY name
#             SQLite's group_concat does NOT support ORDER BY inside the aggregate (unlike
#             MySQL's GROUP_CONCAT … ORDER BY … syntax).  The trailing ORDER BY clause operates
#             on the single aggregate-result row — it is a complete no-op.  Table names were
#             concatenated in physical storage order, which can differ between runs (after INSERT,
#             DELETE, or VACUUM).  Consecutive scan sessions on the same target produced different
#             table orderings, making extraction checkpointing and resume unreliable.
#             Fix: push ORDER BY into a subquery so SQLite sorts rows before group_concat sees them:
#               SELECT group_concat(name,',') FROM (SELECT name FROM sqlite_master WHERE type='table' ORDER BY name)
#
#   BUG-MSSQL-STRINGAGG-NO-WITHIN-GROUP [SchemaEnumeratorV18 — MSSQL row_chunked STRING_AGG
#             without WITHIN GROUP (ORDER BY) produces non-deterministic row aggregation]:
#             ROOT CAUSE: STRING_AGG(v_, sep) without a WITHIN GROUP clause allows SQL Server
#             to aggregate rows in any order it chooses, independent of the outer ORDER BY
#             (SELECT NULL) OFFSET/FETCH clause that controls which rows are in the page.
#             Within each OFFSET/FETCH page the row order was non-deterministic, so dump_rows()
#             could parse rows in a different sequence than they were fetched, producing
#             misaligned column-to-value mappings for multi-column tables.
#             Fix: add WITHIN GROUP (ORDER BY (SELECT NULL)) to match SQL Server's implementation-
#             defined ordering with the inner subquery's ORDER BY (SELECT NULL), giving consistent
#             intra-page row ordering without requiring a primary key column name.
