#             defined ordering with the inner subquery's ORDER BY (SELECT NULL), giving consistent
#             intra-page row ordering without requiring a primary key column name.
#
#   BUG-PG-ENUM-COLS-NO-ORDER [_error_enum_columns — PostgreSQL column query LIMIT 1 OFFSET N
#             without ORDER BY returns duplicate columns]:
#             ROOT CAUSE: Without ORDER BY ordinal_position, consecutive LIMIT 1 OFFSET N calls
#             in the PostgreSQL column-enumeration loop could return the same column twice (if the
#             query planner chose a different row-set order between calls, which can happen when
#             autovacuum or HOT pruning runs between probes).  This caused _error_enum_columns
#             to build a column list with duplicates and miss real columns, silently corrupting
#             the schema enumeration result for PostgreSQL error-based extraction.
#             Fix: add ORDER BY ordinal_position to the col_query_tpl for PostgreSQL so each
#             OFFSET N selects the Nth column in the schema-defined sequence.
#
#   BUG-MYSQL-ENUM-COLS-NO-ORDER [_error_enum_columns — MySQL/MariaDB column query LIMIT
#             {offset},1 without ORDER BY — same non-deterministic issue]:
#             ROOT CAUSE: MySQL/MariaDB LIMIT offset,1 without ORDER BY is non-deterministic
#             (the storage engine can return rows in any order, especially on tables with
#             concurrent writes or after OPTIMIZE TABLE).  Same duplicate-column risk as PostgreSQL.
#             Fix: add ORDER BY ordinal_position to MySQL/MariaDB col_query_tpl.
#
#   BUG-MYSQL-ERROR-TABLES-NO-ORDER [_error_enum_tables — MySQL/MariaDB and PostgreSQL
#             table_query_tpl missing ORDER BY — non-deterministic table enumeration]:
#             ROOT CAUSE: LIMIT {offset},1 (MySQL) and LIMIT 1 OFFSET {offset} (PostgreSQL)
#             without ORDER BY produce non-deterministic results.  Consecutive calls with
#             different offsets may return the same table, causing get_tables() to return a
#             list with duplicated table names and missing tables from the tail of the result.
#             Fix: add ORDER BY table_name / ORDER BY tablename to both DBMSes.
#
#   BUG-SQLITE-ERROR-TABLES-NO-ORDER [_error_enum_tables — SQLite table_query_tpl missing
#             ORDER BY name — non-deterministic table enumeration]:
#             ROOT CAUSE: Same as MySQL/PostgreSQL above.  SQLite's LIMIT 1 OFFSET {offset}
#             without ORDER BY returns rows in B-tree traversal order which changes after
#             any structural modification (INSERT/DELETE/VACUUM).
#             Fix: add ORDER BY name to SQLite table_query_tpl.
#

#             ROOT CAUSE: BitwiseExtractorSimple._extract_char_bits built the bit-probe
#             condition as `({_q})&{mask}={mask}` for every DBMS without exception.
#             Oracle SQL does not support & as a bitwise operator (it is a SQL*Plus line-
#             continuation character); Oracle requires BITAND(expr, mask) = mask.
#             Firebird requires BIN_AND(expr, mask) = mask. When Oracle received the
#             expression it raised ORA-00907 (missing right parenthesis). The eval oracle
#             treats an error response as False, so ALL 8 bit-probes returned 0,
#             char_val = 0, and every extracted character became chr(0) → '□'. The
#             BitwiseExtractorSimple fallback (third rung in _extract_str cascade, called
#             after BatchedCharExtractor fails) was completely non-functional for Oracle
#             and Firebird targets. The same bug existed in the retry_bits loop below the
#             main gather. Both instances fixed with DBMS-aware dispatch.
#             Fix: in _probe_bit and the retry_bits loop, dispatch on self._dbms:
#               Oracle   → f"BITAND({_q},{mask})={mask}"
#               Firebird → f"BIN_AND({_q},{mask})={mask}"
#               all else → f"({_q})&{mask}={mask}"
#
#   BUG-EQUALITY-LATIN1 [_extract_char_equality only checks printable ASCII (32-126),
#             never finding Latin-1 extended chars 128-255 in any DBMS]:
#             ROOT CAUSE: The Scanner's inline _extract_char_equality function iterates
#             `for ch in _FREQ_CHARS` where _FREQ_CHARS is a plain ASCII string covering
#             only code points 32-126. _LATIN1_FREQ (defined just above) holds integer
#             code points for common extended Latin-1 chars (é=233, ü=252, ö=246, ñ=241,
#             ç=231, etc.) and is correctly used by _extract_char_subtraction — but was
#             never converted to character literals for the equality oracle. Any DB value
#             containing accented or extended chars always caused _extract_char_equality
#             to return None, making _extract_string_equality return '' for those positions
#             on every DBMS (MySQL, PostgreSQL, MSSQL, Oracle, SQLite alike).
#             Fix: after the _FREQ_CHARS loop, add a second loop over _LATIN1_FREQ
#             converting each integer to chr() and trying it as a quoted string literal.
#
#   BUG-BETWEEN-HI-ASCII [_CHAR_FN BETWEEN-mode has upper bound AND 126 for PostgreSQL,
#             MySQL, MariaDB, Sybase, DB2, CockroachDB, Firebird, H2, Informix, SAP_HANA —
#             making extraction of all chars > 126 silently wrong when WAF blocks >]:
#             ROOT CAUSE: When _waf_blocks_gt=True (WAF blocks the > comparison operator),
#             extraction switches to BETWEEN mode. The _CHAR_FN dict had:
#               "PostgreSQL": "ASCII(...)  BETWEEN {mid} AND 126"
#               "MySQL":      "ORD(...)    BETWEEN {mid} AND 126"
#               (and similarly for MariaDB, Sybase, DB2, CockroachDB, Firebird, H2,
#                Informix, SAP_HANA — all with AND 126)
#             The binary search in _extract_string/extract_char_at correctly sets
#             lo=32, hi=255, but it computes effective_mid = mid+1 (BETWEEN semantics)
#             and substitutes it into {mid}. Once mid >= 126, the SQL becomes:
#               ASCII(...) BETWEEN 127 AND 126  ← N > M, ALWAYS FALSE in every DBMS
#             The oracle always returns False for any character > 126, driving hi downward
#             until convergence at chr(126)='~'. Every char 127-255 (é, ü, ö, ä, ñ,
#             ç, ï, â, etc.) extracted as '~' on all affected DBMSes when running in
#             BETWEEN mode. Only MSSQL, Oracle, and SQLite had already been fixed to
#             AND 65535; the remaining nine DBMSes were missed.
#             Fix: change AND 126 → AND 255 for all ASCII()-based DBMS entries
#             (PostgreSQL, MySQL, MariaDB, Sybase, DB2, CockroachDB, Firebird, H2,
#              Informix, SAP_HANA). 255 is the correct ceiling for ASCII()/ORD()/
#             ASCII_VAL() functions which return 0-255 by definition.
#
