#   BUG-POSTGRESQL-PCV-CHECKB-VOID [Req 3 — PostgreSQL timing PCV Check B always failed]:
#             _derive_timing_payloads() PostgreSQL template was:
#               "CASE WHEN SUBSTR(...)='ch' THEN pg_sleep(N) ELSE pg_sleep(0) END"
#             ROOT CAUSE: pg_sleep() returns type VOID in PostgreSQL. A CASE expression
#             returning VOID is rejected in a WHERE clause context:
#               ERROR: argument of WHERE must be type boolean, not type void
#             This caused every PostgreSQL timing PCV Check B canary probe to fail with
#             a SQL error, so ALL confirmed PostgreSQL timing injections were permanently
#             rejected by PCV — they were NEVER escalated to SQLi.
#             Fix: changed to:
#               "(CASE WHEN ... THEN (SELECT 1 FROM pg_sleep(N::float)) ELSE 1 END)=1"
#             Uses pg_sleep as a SRF in a FROM clause (valid, returns 1 after sleeping),
#             both branches return integer 1, =1 makes it boolean. Valid in all PG versions.
#
#   BUG-ORACLE-PCV-CHECKB-NUMERIC [Req 3 — Oracle timing PCV Check B always failed]:
#             _derive_timing_payloads() Oracle template was:
#               "CASE WHEN SUBSTR(...)='ch' THEN (SELECT COUNT(*) FROM all_objects ...) ELSE 0 END"
#             ROOT CAUSE: This is a numeric expression. Oracle rejects numeric values in
#             WHERE clause without a comparison operator:
#               ORA-00920: invalid relational operator
#             Every Oracle timing PCV Check B probe failed with this SQL error, so ALL
#             confirmed Oracle timing injections were NEVER escalated to SQLi.
#             Fix: appended >= 0 outside the CASE expression to make it boolean.
#             COUNT(*) is always >= 0, so both canary variants return TRUE; only timing differs.
